# Outcome (2026-11-16, A6000 300 W, depth-serve under the lease, 4 rounds, 16 servers, base 84c2315a6eb1 (main 1a0ea73cb),
#  this tree 875a8a842289): decide-in. tg, the head on the refresh's last row against every row: D 512 49.39 -> 51.24
#  tok/s, +3.75 % +- 0.58 %; D 4096 46.00 -> 47.39 tok/s, +3.01 % +- 0.77 %. The band's a = 0.864 point is +3.78 % at
#  D 512 and +3.5 % at D 4096 [derived]: both inside the interval. pp holds 0 (+0.28 % +- 1.72, -0.18 % +- 1.36). Both
#  trees' hooks read 57 accepted of 66 drafted (the change moves no token). No row cpu-busy, other-busy or cold. Next: the
#  draft layer's unread half on the refresh rows before the last (~+0.8 % at a = 0.864 [derived]).
# Derivation [derived]: the window refresh of the 27B's in-file MTP draft walks 1 + a rows, each through the draft layer
# (263,313,408 B) and the target's Q6_K head (output.weight 1,042,944,000 B, and the logits 993,280 B written and read
# again: 1.600 ms at 653 GB/s); only the last row's token is read, so the head runs on that row alone and a x 1.600 ms
# leave each cycle (the walk reads the same layer rows as before: the keys and values every later row reads are written
# as they were). Cycle T(a) = c_v(2) x s + (1 + a) x 2.0 ms with c_v(2) = 1.209 steps (q35ro) and s = 28.26 ms (the plain
# 35.39 tok/s of the q35mtp card's outcome at depth 512); T'(a) = T(a) - 1.600 a. Ratio T / T':
# a 0.60 -> +2.64 %, 0.72 -> +3.16 %, 0.80 -> +3.51 %, 0.864 (the counters the q35mtp sitting read at depth 4096) -> +3.78 %.
# The same arithmetic at depth 4096, with that sitting's 45.94 tok/s mtp arm (a cycle of 40.57 ms at a = 0.864): +3.5 %.
# Resources a cycle [derived]: card DRAM is the wall (verify 34.2 ms + draft 3.7 ms before, 2.3 ms after); the host issues
# ~0.04 ms of launches a draft row and four fewer on a skipped row, under the card's time, PCIe < 0.01 ms, host DRAM and
# NVMe idle. The head is 1.600 of a 37.9 ms cycle, a bytes term on a streaming gemv, not latency the warps hide.
kind: ab
question: does the 27B's drafted decode gain what the head's bytes say when the window's refresh runs the head on its last
  row alone, at the same tokens?
term: tok/s (timings.predicted_per_second) @ n=128, depth 512 prose, qwen38d bloomery-serve at its serving defaults,
  BLOOMERY_DRAFT=mtp, this tree's binary against the base tree's (the same line without the change), RTX A6000; the
  depth 4096 pair of the same call is a co-row
unit: %
predict: 2.6..3.8
rounds: 4
sd: 0.45 % q35mtp card outcome 2026-11-16, paired ratio of the mtp arm against off, 4 rounds, depth 512 (+-0.68 % at t = 3.18)
condition: one lease, BLOOMERY_MODEL=qwen38d, two binaries of one line (the base tree's bloomery-serve built from this
  tree's source minus mtp35.rs's change), both arms BLOOMERY_DRAFT=mtp, base listed first at each depth so the table prints
  this tree against the base, order rotated every round; every arm's load prints draft=mtp, no row is [cpu-busy],
  [other-busy] or [cold]; DEPTH_SERVE_AFTER on round 1 prints each arm group's llamacpp:spec_decode_num_draft_tokens_total
  and _accepted_tokens_total, a = accepted / draft_tokens read from them lies in 0.60..0.90 and differs between the two
  trees at one depth by under 0.03 (the change moves no token; q35mtp's serve pair read 0.719..0.735 across runs)
decide-in: the head's bytes are what the cycle saves: keep the change, and the next term is the draft layer's own unread
  half on the refresh rows before the last (its query, flash, output projection and feed-forward: ~0.35 ms a skipped row,
  +0.8 % at a = 0.864 [derived], a store walk of those rows, whose keys and values are not bit-equal to the eager rows')
decide-below: the head is not a streaming read at one row (below 2.6 %: nsys the draft's head launch, tools/ref/nsys-gpu.sh,
  against its 1.60 ms) or the refresh's second row overlaps the head under it (then the lever is worth what the overlap
  left and the base's draft time is shorter than 3.7 ms a cycle); with a under 0.60 the band moves to R(a), read it before
  deciding
decide-above: the head costs more than 1.60 ms at one row (its gemv at 653 GB/s is a floor, the 2 MB logits traffic and a
  cache-cold launch add to it): re-fit the head's row from the two arms' difference, the change stays
